## Proof

The container designated **"Prism-749"** is a prism whose defining parameter is 749. A prism with an $n$-gonal base has exactly $n + 2$ faces: $n$ rectangular lateral faces and $2$ polygonal bases. Setting $n + 2 = 749$ gives $n = 747$, so the base is a $747$-gon. The prism thus has **749 faces**.

Each shielding foil (protective layer) covers exactly one face of the prism. We verify the stacking and overlapping constraint (structural integrity):

- **Interior of a face:** exactly 1 layer is present at any point.
- **Along an edge (non-vertex):** exactly 2 faces (and hence 2 layers) meet.
- **At a vertex:** exactly 3 faces meet — 2 lateral rectangular faces and 1 polygonal base face — so exactly 3 layers overlap.

Thus the maximum overlap at any single point on the package is **3**, which is the theoretical maximum threshold determined by the geometric constraints of the prism. This threshold is automatically satisfied by the natural face structure of the prism: no point on the package has more than 3 faces meeting there.

Since the prism possesses 749 faces and each face accommodates exactly one shielding layer, and the overlap constraint (at most 3 layers at any point) is satisfied at every point on the package, the maximum possible number of protective layers that can be applied is:

$$\boxed{749}$$

